1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
This is Doing and
Feeling, the long version.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,360
Episode five: The Predictive Brain.

3
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:09,790
Take a hollow mask of a face and turn
it so you're looking at its inside, the

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:12,440
concave side. You'd
expect to see a hollow.

5
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:17,570
Most people don't. They see a normal
face bulging toward them, and if the mask

6
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:21,500
rotates, the face seems to turn the
wrong way, eerily tracking them.

7
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:23,830
Knowing that it's hollow doesn't help.

8
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:29,630
Your brain has an extremely strong expectation
that faces are convex, and the light

9
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:33,460
reaching your eyes is ambiguous
enough that the expectation wins.

10
00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:38,910
In one small brain-imaging study from
2009, with thirteen patients and sixteen

11
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:43,890
controls, healthy volunteers were fooled
almost every time, while people with

12
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:46,340
schizophrenia were fooled far less often.

13
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:51,650
It's a small sample, but it fits the idea
that the balance between expectation and

14
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:54,620
evidence is a setting, and
that it can be set differently.

15
00:00:54,960 --> 00:00:59,590
This episode argues that the brain is well
described as a prediction machine, and

16
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:03,320
then shows where that description is
solid and where it is still mostly slogan.

17
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:06,080
We'll put the idea through five tests:

18
00:01:06,420 --> 00:01:12,270
perception, the wiring of the cortex, a
grand unifying principle, reward, and the

19
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:17,150
brain's internal maps. It's the bridge
between the computational story of the last

20
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,080
episode and the machines of the next.

21
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:24,680
The modern form of the idea starts with
Hermann von Helmholtz in the eighteen

22
00:01:24,730 --> 00:01:29,120
sixties. Perception, he argued,
involves unconscious inference:

23
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:34,750
the brain receives ambiguous signals and
infers their most probable causes, drawing

24
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:38,060
on experience. The
modern form is Bayesian.

25
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:42,610
Combine a likelihood, how well each
possible cause would explain the incoming

26
00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:47,710
signals, with a prior, how probable each
cause was to begin with, and you get a

27
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:49,880
posterior, the brain's best guess.

28
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:54,880
The hollow mask is what happens when the
prior is strong and the likelihood is weak.

29
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:56,960
Is the brain actually Bayesian?

30
00:01:57,180 --> 00:02:00,660
The best evidence comes from
experiments on combining the senses.

31
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:06,530
In 2002, Marc Ernst and Martin Banks had
people judge the height of a bar they could

32
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:11,830
both see and feel. When the researchers
added noise to the visual display, people

33
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:16,590
leaned more on touch, and they weighted
the two senses almost exactly as an ideal

34
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:19,980
statistician would, in proportion
to each one's reliability.

35
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:24,580
But there is a long list of cases where
people are measurably not optimal.

36
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:31,430
In 2018, Dobromir Rahnev and Rachel Denison
catalogued them in a target article for

37
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:36,390
the journal Behavioral and Brain Sciences,
and argued that claims of optimality are

38
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:38,840
often flexible enough
to fit almost anything.

39
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:43,550
Their advice was to stop asking whether
the brain is optimal and start building

40
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:46,340
explicit models of what
observers actually do.

41
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:51,890
In that journal, a target article is published
together with many short commentaries

42
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:56,910
from other researchers and a reply from
the authors, which is a good way to see how

43
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:59,260
contested even this basic claim is.

44
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:01,980
So is the brain Bayesian or not?

45
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:07,220
The fairest answer is that it uses priors
and weights evidence by reliability,

46
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:10,280
approximately, with systematic shortcuts.

47
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:14,280
Bayesian is a good framework for
asking what the brain is computing.

48
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:17,740
It is not a finding that the
brain computes it perfectly.

49
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:21,340
The second test is the cortex itself.

50
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:27,750
In 1999, Rajesh Rao and Dana Ballard
proposed an influential model of predictive

51
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:31,940
coding in visual cortex. The
visual system is a hierarchy.

52
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:36,650
In their model, each level sends predictions
down about what the level below should

53
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:40,990
be seeing, and sends up only the prediction
error, the part of the signal that

54
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:45,260
wasn't predicted. When predictions are
good, little needs to travel upward.

55
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:51,110
Their model explained puzzling properties
of neurons in visual cortex, such as cells

56
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:55,890
that respond less to a line when it extends
beyond the patch they monitor, because

57
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:58,400
the extension was
predictable from context.

58
00:03:58,680 --> 00:04:00,300
That's a compression scheme.

59
00:04:00,720 --> 00:04:02,600
Video codecs do the same thing:

60
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:06,370
send keyframes, then only the
differences from what you'd predict.

61
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:11,550
And the parallel with episode three's
equivalence of prediction and compression is

62
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:15,500
no accident. How much of what
comes in actually reaches behavior?

63
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:21,730
In a 2024 paper, Jieyu Zheng and Markus
Meister estimated that human behavior

64
00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:26,530
carries about ten bits of information per
second, while our senses take in something

65
00:04:26,580 --> 00:04:30,380
like a billion. They called the gap
the unbearable slowness of being.

66
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:35,610
The authors stress how little anyone
understands about that bottleneck, but it is at

67
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:40,430
least consistent with a brain that keeps
a model of its world and passes on mainly

68
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:42,300
what the model failed to anticipate.

69
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,680
The supporting evidence for
predictive coding is real.

70
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:51,490
Neural responses shrink when a stimulus
repeats, and in 2008, Christopher

71
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:56,390
Summerfield and colleagues showed with
functional MRI that this repetition

72
00:04:56,440 --> 00:05:00,790
suppression in a face-processing region
was stronger when repeats were expected.

73
00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:06,210
A well-known brainwave, the mismatch
negativity, appears when a run of identical

74
00:05:06,260 --> 00:05:08,500
tones is broken by a deviant one.

75
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:13,890
And if you omit a tone from a predictable
sequence, auditory cortex responds to the

76
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:17,900
silence. In predictive coding,
attention becomes precision:

77
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:22,570
the weight the brain gives to prediction
errors from a source, depending on how

78
00:05:22,620 --> 00:05:24,940
reliable that source is expected to be.

79
00:05:25,340 --> 00:05:31,330
Now the mess. When Dzmitry Kaliukhovich
and Rufin Vogels recorded single neurons in

80
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:36,070
the monkey's temporal cortex, repetition
suppression did not depend on how likely

81
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:40,890
the repeat was. Summerfield's own group
later called the mismatch between the brain

82
00:05:40,940 --> 00:05:43,180
scans and the neurons unexplained.

83
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:45,460
And there's a live dispute right now.

84
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:51,890
A large preprint led by Jacob Westerberg
and André Bastos, recording from mice and

85
00:05:51,940 --> 00:05:57,290
monkeys, reported that most oddball
responses in sensory cortex are explained by

86
00:05:57,340 --> 00:06:01,180
adaptation, neurons simply
tiring of repeated input.

87
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:06,830
Responses to violations of a learned
sequence were sparse, came late, and, in the

88
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:11,750
monkeys, appeared first in prefrontal
cortex, which suggests the deep prediction

89
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:15,080
errors may be computed higher
up rather than at every level.

90
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:21,710
Then, in January 2026, another preprint,
from Jordan Hamm's lab, found that primary

91
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:27,450
visual cortex in mice does respond to
sequence-level surprises, learned within fewer

92
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:31,750
than ten repetitions, and that the effect
vanished when feedback from a frontal

93
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:35,020
region, the anterior
cingulate, was silenced.

94
00:06:35,220 --> 00:06:39,520
Both were posted as preprints, and they
may be measuring somewhat different things.

95
00:06:39,660 --> 00:06:41,820
But notice what is being argued about.

96
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:44,920
Nobody in this fight doubts
that the brain predicts.

97
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:50,910
In 2025, the International Brain Laboratory
published a brain-wide map of mice

98
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:55,870
making decisions, recorded from more than
half a million neurons across two hundred

99
00:06:55,920 --> 00:06:57,380
and seventy-nine brain areas.

100
00:06:57,560 --> 00:07:02,170
It found the animals' prior expectations
encoded in at least a fifth to a third of

101
00:07:02,220 --> 00:07:07,790
the regions, at every level from early
sensory areas, including the thalamus, up to

102
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:13,170
motor and frontal cortex. What's in question
is the specific textbook circuit, in

103
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:16,020
which every level computes
errors and passes them up.

104
00:07:16,920 --> 00:07:20,260
The third test is the
grandest version of the idea.

105
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:25,710
Starting in the mid two thousands, the
neuroscientist Karl Friston proposed the free

106
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:28,400
energy principle. Here's the core logic.

107
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:34,150
Any system that persists, a cell, an
organism, a brain, must stay within a limited

108
00:07:34,200 --> 00:07:36,500
set of states compatible
with its existence.

109
00:07:36,860 --> 00:07:38,740
A fish must stay in water.

110
00:07:39,140 --> 00:07:43,090
In information-theoretic terms,
it must avoid surprising states.

111
00:07:43,140 --> 00:07:48,150
But a system can't measure surprise directly,
because that would require knowing the

112
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:49,820
true probability of everything.

113
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:55,170
What it can do is minimize a quantity
called variational free energy, which is

114
00:07:55,220 --> 00:07:57,200
always at least as large as surprise.

115
00:07:57,860 --> 00:08:00,720
Keep free energy low and
you keep surprise low.

116
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:04,600
That's the evidence lower bound
from variational inference.

117
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:07,160
Free energy is just its negative.

118
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:09,540
Exactly the same mathematics.

119
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:12,200
Minimizing it does two things at once:

120
00:08:12,380 --> 00:08:16,930
it makes the system's approximate posterior
closer to the true one, and it improves

121
00:08:16,980 --> 00:08:19,280
how well the system's model fits its data.

122
00:08:19,740 --> 00:08:24,630
In a variational autoencoder, an encoder
network learns to produce that approximate

123
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:26,460
posterior in a single pass.

124
00:08:26,900 --> 00:08:31,560
Predictive coding settles on it gradually,
by passing errors back and forth.

125
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:33,920
Friston adds two big ideas.

126
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:39,110
The first is the Markov blanket, a
statistical boundary that separates a system's

127
00:08:39,160 --> 00:08:43,590
internal states from the world, mediated
by sensory states coming in and active

128
00:08:43,640 --> 00:08:46,880
states going out. The
second is active inference.

129
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:50,480
An organism can reduce
prediction error in two ways:

130
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:55,470
change its predictions to fit the world,
which is perception, or change the world to

131
00:08:55,520 --> 00:08:57,500
fit its predictions, which is action.

132
00:08:57,740 --> 00:09:02,130
When you reach for a cup, on this view,
your brain predicts the sensations of

133
00:09:02,180 --> 00:09:05,740
grasping it, and your muscles act
to make that prediction come true.

134
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:11,270
To choose actions, agents score them by
a related quantity called expected free

135
00:09:11,320 --> 00:09:15,400
energy, which combines getting what you
prefer with reducing your uncertainty.

136
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:19,380
That second part is a
principled account of curiosity.

137
00:09:19,860 --> 00:09:22,620
There's a classic objection
called the dark room problem.

138
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:27,760
If organisms minimize surprise, why
don't we all sit in a dark, quiet room?

139
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:33,090
The standard answer, from a 2012 paper
by Friston, Christopher Thornton and Andy

140
00:09:33,140 --> 00:09:37,360
Clark, is that surprise is relative
to an organism's model of itself.

141
00:09:37,700 --> 00:09:43,190
A creature that expects to eat, stay warm
and explore would find a dark room deeply

142
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:47,020
surprising. The principle
has serious critics.

143
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:53,190
If minimizing free energy covers perception,
action, attention and learning all at

144
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:56,440
once, what observation
could ever count against it?

145
00:09:56,700 --> 00:10:00,820
That's the first criticism, and
Friston largely agrees, in a sense.

146
00:10:01,020 --> 00:10:05,710
He describes it as a mathematical principle,
like the principle of least action in

147
00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:09,970
physics, from which testable process
theories such as predictive coding can be

148
00:10:10,020 --> 00:10:14,850
derived. Critics reply that a framework
that can describe anything explains nothing

149
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:19,010
until you commit to a specific process
theory, and the process theories are where

150
00:10:19,060 --> 00:10:22,900
the evidence lives, and where,
as we just saw, the fights are.

151
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:29,030
The second is technical. In a 2022
Behavioral and Brain Sciences target article

152
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:34,330
titled The Emperor's New Markov Blankets,
Jelle Bruineberg and colleagues argued

153
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:37,620
that the field uses Markov
blankets in two senses.

154
00:10:37,860 --> 00:10:41,860
One is a formal tool from
statistics, a property of a model.

155
00:10:42,080 --> 00:10:46,970
The other is a claim about real physical
boundaries between organisms and their

156
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:52,210
environments. Sliding between them, they
argue, makes the framework seem to deliver

157
00:10:52,260 --> 00:10:57,050
more than it does. The third is a tension
with reinforcement learning, which says

158
00:10:57,100 --> 00:11:02,810
agents maximize reward. Friston's reply
is that rewards can be recast as prior

159
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:08,260
preferences: the organism expects to
be fed, so being fed is unsurprising.

160
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:13,550
Whether that recasting adds insight or
just relabels the problem is still argued

161
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:19,490
about. The fourth test is reward, and here
the brain offers what may be the clearest

162
00:11:19,540 --> 00:11:22,700
machine learning algorithm
ever found in biology.

163
00:11:23,080 --> 00:11:27,770
In the nineteen nineties, Wolfram Schultz
recorded dopamine neurons in monkeys

164
00:11:27,820 --> 00:11:30,080
learning that a cue
predicted a squirt of juice.

165
00:11:30,380 --> 00:11:33,880
At first, the neurons fired a
burst when the juice arrived.

166
00:11:34,300 --> 00:11:38,830
After learning, the burst moved to the
cue, and the juice itself produced no

167
00:11:38,880 --> 00:11:43,710
response. And if the expected juice
failed to arrive, the neurons dipped below

168
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:46,240
baseline at exactly the
moment it should have come.

169
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:52,030
In 1997, Schultz, Peter Dayan and Read
Montague pointed out that this is precisely

170
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:56,470
the reward prediction error of temporal
difference learning, an algorithm Richard

171
00:11:56,520 --> 00:11:58,280
Sutton had developed for machines:

172
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:03,270
the reward you got, plus the value of
where you ended up, minus the value you

173
00:12:03,320 --> 00:12:08,560
predicted. Once the cue reliably predicts
juice, the juice is no longer news.

174
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:11,900
The news arrives with the
cue, and so does the burst.

175
00:12:12,180 --> 00:12:17,090
The same algorithm had powered TD-Gammon,
a backgammon program from the early

176
00:12:17,140 --> 00:12:19,700
nineties. The correspondence has deepened.

177
00:12:20,060 --> 00:12:25,250
In 2020, Will Dabney and colleagues at
DeepMind and Harvard found that dopamine

178
00:12:25,300 --> 00:12:28,960
neurons encode not one expected
reward but a distribution.

179
00:12:29,380 --> 00:12:35,110
Some neurons are tuned to be optimistic,
others pessimistic, and the population as a

180
00:12:35,160 --> 00:12:40,250
whole represents the range of possible
rewards, exactly as in distributional

181
00:12:40,300 --> 00:12:44,240
reinforcement learning, which had been
invented a few years earlier for AI.

182
00:12:44,760 --> 00:12:50,390
An algorithm first tested on Atari games
turned out to fit dopamine recordings in

183
00:12:50,440 --> 00:12:56,250
mice. That could mean the brain runs it,
or just that evolution and engineers found

184
00:12:56,300 --> 00:13:00,120
the same good solution. Either
way, the match keeps growing.

185
00:13:00,630 --> 00:13:06,970
In 2025, a study in Nature reported that
dopamine neurons jointly map when a reward

186
00:13:07,020 --> 00:13:08,880
will arrive and how big it will be.

187
00:13:09,100 --> 00:13:11,500
But even here the slogan has a challenger.

188
00:13:11,860 --> 00:13:18,170
In 2022, Huijeong Jeong, Vijay Namboodiri
and colleagues argued, from experiments in

189
00:13:18,220 --> 00:13:23,220
mice, that dopamine release looks more like
a signal of learned causal associations,

190
00:13:23,560 --> 00:13:27,720
worked out by looking backward from
rewards, than a forward prediction error.

191
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:33,430
The prediction-error account remains the
best-supported algorithm in biology, and it

192
00:13:33,480 --> 00:13:35,100
is still contested at the edges.

193
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:42,730
The fifth test is maps. In 1948, Edward
Tolman argued from experiments with rats in

194
00:13:42,780 --> 00:13:47,110
mazes that animals build cognitive
maps, internal models that let them take

195
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:48,660
shortcuts they've never tried.

196
00:13:49,060 --> 00:13:55,230
The neural evidence arrived in 1971, when
John O'Keefe and Jonathan Dostrovsky found

197
00:13:55,280 --> 00:14:00,030
place cells in the rat hippocampus,
neurons that fire when the animal is in a

198
00:14:00,080 --> 00:14:05,730
particular location. In 2005, May-Britt
and Edvard Moser's lab found grid cells in

199
00:14:05,780 --> 00:14:10,550
the nearby entorhinal cortex, which fire
at many locations arranged in a strikingly

200
00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:14,410
regular triangular lattice, like
coordinate lines laid over the world.

201
00:14:14,460 --> 00:14:18,500
O'Keefe and the Mosers
shared the 2014 Nobel Prize.

202
00:14:19,240 --> 00:14:23,440
Nearby are head-direction cells, which
work like a compass, and boundary cells,

203
00:14:23,640 --> 00:14:28,220
which fire near walls. Together they form
something close to a navigation system.

204
00:14:28,500 --> 00:14:33,930
These maps predict. During rest and sleep,
the hippocampus replays sequences of

205
00:14:33,980 --> 00:14:38,910
place cell activity, compressed in time,
including paths the animal took and

206
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:40,610
sometimes paths it hasn't.

207
00:14:40,660 --> 00:14:47,070
In 2017, Kimberly Stachenfeld, Matthew
Botvinick and Samuel Gershman proposed that

208
00:14:47,120 --> 00:14:51,870
the hippocampus encodes each place by where
you are likely to go next from it, what

209
00:14:51,920 --> 00:14:54,880
reinforcement learning calls
a successor representation.

210
00:14:55,360 --> 00:14:59,860
That explains why place fields stretch
backward along a habitual route.

211
00:15:00,020 --> 00:15:01,900
And the maps aren't only spatial.

212
00:15:02,220 --> 00:15:08,270
In 2016, Alexandra Constantinescu, Jill
O'Reilly and Timothy Behrens taught people

213
00:15:08,320 --> 00:15:13,370
an abstract space of cartoon birds whose
necks and legs varied in length, and found

214
00:15:13,420 --> 00:15:18,040
grid-like brain signals as people mentally
moved through that conceptual space.

215
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:23,580
The brain seems to reuse its navigation
machinery to organize knowledge in general.

216
00:15:23,960 --> 00:15:28,350
James Whittington, Behrens and
colleagues then built a model called the

217
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:32,990
Tolman-Eichenbaum Machine that learns to
separate the structure of an environment

218
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:37,830
from its sensory contents, so the same
